* Dogfood task 2026-07-18: device descriptor pairing dogfood-driver-
* loopback.a's "dogloop" driver with the SCF file manager, per
* os9-systems-dev's device-drivers.md Device Descriptor Fields table.
* Full account: dogfood-report-driver-2026-07-18.md.
*
* VERIFICATION STATUS:
*   - Assembles clean (0 errors) once string literals use double
*     quotes -- r68 rejects single-quoted `dc.b 'SCF'` outright with
*     "*** error - value out of range ***" on EVERY single-quoted
*     string tried (2/3-char and even/odd length), while `dc.b "SCF"`
*     (double quotes) assembles with no complaint. Not documented
*     anywhere surveyed; found by a minimal isolated probe this
*     session after the same error showed up on both string fields
*     below. Worth folding into the skill's toolchain notes.
*   - Links clean (0 errors), produces a genuine CRC-valid module:
*     `ident` reports "68000 Dev Descr, Object Code, Sharable, System
*     State Process", ty/la=$F01 (Devic/Objct), attr/rev=$A001.
*   - CONFIRMED LIVE, GENUINE TOOLCHAIN GAP (not just "unattested"):
*     the 6-operand psect form is the *only* form r68 accepts for a
*     module header (a 4-operand form omitting stacksize/entry fails
*     "*** error - comma expected ***"), and it unconditionally
*     reserves 12 bytes matching mod_exec's M$Exec/M$Excpt/M$Data
*     slots before the psect body -- regardless of module type and
*     regardless of what literal values are passed for those operands.
*     Confirmed by dumping this file's own linked module: every field
*     below (M$Port through M$Opt) is internally self-consistent
*     (M$FMgr/M$PDev correctly point at "SCF"/"dogloop" wherever the
*     linker actually put them) but landed 12 bytes LATER than
*     module_from_book.h's documented mod_dev offsets ($30 is not
*     really M$Port; add $0C for the true file offset). No shorter
*     psect form, and no separate directive for non-mod_exec-shaped
*     module types (Devic/Data), was found in the skill tree or by
*     direct experiment. This means device-drivers.md's Device
*     Descriptor Fields table (M$Port @ $30, M$Mode @ $37, etc.) is
*     NOT currently hand-authorable byte-accurately with the one
*     working psect form this session could find.
*   - The module's registered name (M$Name, what F$Link/F$Load use to
*     find it by device name) comes from l68's `-o=<name>` OUTPUT
*     FILE argument, NOT from this source's `nam`/`psect` name
*     operands -- confirmed by linking this file first as
*     `l68 -o=dogfoodmod dogfood.r` (module registered under the name
*     "dogfoodmod", per `mdir` and a `dump` of its M$Name string) and
*     then relinking as `l68 -o=DOGFOOD dogfood.r` (module then
*     registered as "DOGFOOD", exactly matching what `/DOGFOOD` path
*     resolution would need to find it by name). Not documented
*     anywhere surveyed; a real, actionable toolchain finding.
*   - Even loaded resident under the exact matching name "DOGFOOD",
*     `dir /DOGFOOD` and a real program's `I$Open("/DOGFOOD")` both
*     still fail with "Module Not Found" (E$MNF) -- os9exec's path
*     resolution for a `/NAME` device path never consults the module
*     directory or F$Link for an arbitrary custom name at all; only a
*     small fixed set of hardcoded path-type names/prefixes is
*     recognized (confirmed against Source/OS9exec_core/utilstuff.c's
*     IO_Type()). `iniz DOGFOOD` also silently no-ops (no error, no
*     `devs` entry ever appears) -- consistent with I$Attach being an
*     explicit dummy in os9exec (icalls.c). Full source citations in
*     the report.
* ============================================================

 nam dogfood
 ttl DOGFOOD device descriptor -- pairs dogloop driver with SCF

* ============================================================
* Device descriptor (module type Devic=15). Its extended header
* ($030 on) is a COMPLETELY different shape from a program's
* (mod_dev vs mod_exec in os9exec's own module_from_book.h) --
* M$Port/M$Vector/M$IRQLvl/M$Prior/M$Mode/M$FMgr/M$PDev/M$DevCon/
* reserved/M$Opt, not M$Exec/M$Excpt/M$Data/M$Stack/M$IData/
* M$IRefs. See the top-of-file verification-status note for the
* confirmed 12-byte psect-header-padding gap this runs into.
* ============================================================
Devic    set $0F
Objct    set $01
ReEnt    set $80
SuperSt  set $20
Type_Lang set (Devic<<8)+Objct
Attr_Rev  set ((ReEnt+SuperSt)<<8)+1

 psect dogfood,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,1,0,0

* Bytes from here on are emitted starting wherever the psect body
* actually lands (measured $3C this session, NOT the canonical
* $30 -- see top-of-file note). Field order/sizes below match
* mod_dev's documented layout; only the true FILE offset each
* field lands at is shifted from the canonical numbers in the
* comments.
dogfood:
 dc.l  0            * $030 M$Port    -- no real hardware, 0
 dc.b  0            * $034 M$Vector  -- no IRQ
 dc.b  0            * $035 M$IRQLvl
 dc.b  0            * $036 M$Prior
 dc.b  $03          * $037 M$Mode: bit0 read + bit1 write

* Bare label references (not label-minus-label arithmetic) --
* live-verified against dogloop.a's own jump table (see that
* file's header comment): the linker resolves a bare dc.w symbol
* reference to that symbol's true module-relative offset
* directly, sidestepping any guess about where the psect body
* landed.
 dc.w  fmgrname   * $038 M$FMgr -- offset to "SCF" string
 dc.w  drvname    * $03A M$PDev -- offset to "dogloop" string
 dc.w  0                  * $03C M$DevCon -- no config table
 dc.l  0                  * $03E..$041 reserved (first 4 of 8)
 dc.l  0                  * $042..$045 reserved (second 4 of 8)
 dc.w  0                  * $046 M$Opt -- 0-byte options table

* NOTE: single-quoted string literals ('SCF', 'dogloop') failed to
* assemble on this toolchain ("*** error - value out of range
* ***") -- double-quoted strings are what actually works, see the
* top-of-file verification-status note.
fmgrname:
 dc.b  "SCF"
 dc.b  0
drvname:
 dc.b  "dogloop"
 dc.b  0

 ends
